{"content":"# Inline review comment — PR #793

**File:** `js/common/trackers.js`

💡 **SUGGESTION: `_jiraView` is a no-op — `toIssueView` already contains this exact passthrough**

The PR description calls `_jiraView` a "Jira view passthrough fix … so field-subset reads stay unchanged", but `toIssueView` (unchanged by this PR, lines ~755–777) already handles it:

```js
if (t && typeof t === 'object' && t.fields && t.fields.summary !== undefined) return t;
// Jira payload fetched with a fields filter (e.g. fields:['Description']) carries no summary —
// still a Jira payload: pass it through unchanged (ADO items always carry System.Title).
if (t && typeof t === 'object' && !Array.isArray(t) && t.fields && typeof t.fields === 'object' &&
    t.fields['System.Title'] === undefined) return t;
```

`_jiraView` re-implements the second condition and otherwise delegates to `toIssueView`, so the two are behaviorally identical for every input (I verified this empirically across Jira-shaped, ADO-shaped, field-subset, string, array and scalar inputs — zero divergence, including the exact `fields: ['key','status']` case this PR targets).

The whole addition — `_jiraView`, the `viewFn` parameter on `_issueList`, and the two call-site changes — therefore changes no behavior and just adds a second copy of the shape-detection heuristic to keep in sync.

**Suggested fix:** delete `_jiraView`, revert `_issueList`/`jiraSearchIssues`/`jiraGetIssue` to use `toIssueView` directly, and (if the intent was to document the field-subset contract) extend the existing `toIssueView` doc comment instead. The new unit tests can keep asserting passthrough behavior — they will still pass against `toIssueView`.

Minor related caveat worth a comment on `toIssueView`: an **ADO** item fetched with a field subset that omits `System.Title` also matches the passthrough and is returned un-normalized (`fields.status` will be `undefined` for readers like `findPendingBugKey`). If ADO reads with `fields:['key','status']` are planned, the heuristic should key off something more specific than "no `System.Title`".
