{"content":"# Inline review comment — PR #793

**File:** `js/createQuestionsAndAssignForReview.js` (same pattern in `postBulkBugsCreation.js`, `postStoryTestAutomationResults.js`)

💡 **SUGGESTION: module-scope singleton tracker is inconsistent with the other 8 scripts in this wave and is a latent wrong-provider footgun**

This PR uses two different wiring styles for the same thing:

- **explicit parameter** (8 scripts, e.g. `checkStoryTestsPassed.js`, `dfManager.js`, `createRepoTasks.js`, `prepareBugFixBatchContext.js`, `unblockResolvedDependencies.js`): `var tracker = trackersModule.createTracker(config, customParams)` passed down to helpers — easy to trace, impossible to use before initialization;
- **module singleton** (these 3 scripts): `initTracker()/getTracker()` over module-scope `_trackerCtx`/`_trackerInst`.

The singleton works today (every `action()` calls `initTracker` before any `getTracker`), but it has a sharp edge: `getTracker()` lazily creates with `{ config: null, customParams: {} }` if ever reached before `initTracker` — which silently resolves to the **jira** provider. On an ADO/GitHub deployment that means the wrong `*_` tool family gets called with no error pointing at the actual mistake, and nothing at module scope warns about it. It also makes the helpers non-reentrant for tests that load the module once and run `action()` twice with different configs.

**Suggested fix:** standardize on the parameter-passing style used by the rest of the wave — thread `tracker` into the module-level helpers (`linkBugToTC`, `postComment`, `removeAutomationLabels`, …) the way `dfManager.buildContext` does (`context.tracker`), and drop `initTracker/getTracker` entirely. If the singleton is kept deliberately (e.g. to avoid touching many helper signatures in one PR), at least guard it:

```js
function getTracker() {
    if (!_trackerInst) {
        if (!_trackerCtx.initialized) throw new Error('initTracker(config, customParams) must run before getTracker()');
        _trackerInst = trackersModule.createTracker(_trackerCtx.config, _trackerCtx.customParams);
    }
    return _trackerInst;
}
```

so an ordering mistake fails loudly instead of silently targeting Jira.
